          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"translation_en\":\"CHRISTIAN (sits down beside her on the bench. A silence):\\n  **I love you.**\",\"larger_context_summary\":\"Christian addresses Roxane during their courtship, attempting to speak to her without Cyrano’s verbal assistance. Roxane believes Christian to possess the eloquence that has actually been supplied by Cyrano through letters and coached speeches, so she expects an elevated expression of passion. Christian’s blunt sentence, delivered after he sits beside her and pauses, disappoints that expectation. The immediate stakes are whether he can sustain Roxane’s love in his own person and words; the wider dramatic irony is that Cyrano, who secretly loves Roxane, has created much of the language through which she understands Christian.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The utterance straightforwardly asserts Christian’s present emotional state, making its truth-presenting force especially clear. Its dramatic intensity does not by itself make it expressive in the narrower, reflexive sense: the preceding silence and composed grammatical form suggest a deliberate avowal rather than an involuntary outburst. Although saying this during courtship can alter expectations and deepen intimacy, the sentence does not independently promise marriage, establish a new status, or otherwise perform a conventional relational act. The wider deception is important but external to the line’s core semantics. Roxane is mistaken about the authorship of Christian’s supposed eloquence, yet Christian’s own declaration need not be false or manipulative. Indeed, its unadorned sincerity is precisely what makes it rhetorically unsuccessful beside Cyrano’s language.\"}"
        }
